{
    "title": "Fisheries of the Exclusive Economic Zone Off Alaska; Reallocation of Pacific Cod in the Bering Sea and Aleutian Islands Management Area",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 30, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-30/pdf/2025-24020.pdf",
    "summary": "The federal government just approved two targeted adjustments to keep U.S. fisheries on track: a small bluefish quota transfer between states on the East Coast and a shift in who can catch some Pacific cod in Alaska so the season\u2019s limits can be fully used.\n\nOn the Atlantic side, New Jersey will transfer 50,000 pounds of bluefish to North Carolina to help North Carolina avoid going over its 2025 limit. After the change, New Jersey\u2019s share is 182,630 pounds and North Carolina\u2019s is 1,472,012 pounds. This kind of state-to-state adjustment is allowed when it helps use the overall annual limit responsibly and reflects real-world changes on the water. It benefits North Carolina\u2019s commercial fleet and the communities that rely on those landings, while New Jersey\u2019s boats give up a modest amount of potential catch.\n\nIn Alaska, managers are moving 930 metric tons of Pacific cod that jig boats were unlikely to catch in the early part of the 2026 season to smaller catcher vessels under 60 feet that use hook-and-line or pot gear. In plain terms, fish that one group wasn\u2019t going to catch will go to boats that can, so the season\u2019s cod limit doesn\u2019t go unused. This helps small-boat fleets and processors that depend on steady supply. On the other hand, jig vessels see their early-season opportunity reduced if conditions change and they could have fished more later.\n\nBoth decisions were made under existing federal fishery rules, with the aim of using science and up-to-date catch data to make timely tweaks. Officials waived a public comment period to act quickly based on data available just before the holidays, which helps avoid waste but may frustrate those who prefer more time to weigh in. Overall, the moves are about matching fishing opportunity with real capacity while staying within sustainable catch limits set for the year.",
    "embedding": [
